@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Input Parameters - SQL Server";
}

<div class="container-fluid py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">
        <h1 class="fw-bold text-primary">Input Parameters</h1>

        <p class="lead">
            Input parameters allow a Stored Procedure to receive values
            from the user or application at runtime.
        </p>
    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                What are Input Parameters?
            </h2>

            <p>
                Input parameters are values passed to a Stored Procedure
                when it is executed.
            </p>

            <p>
                They make Stored Procedures reusable because the same
                procedure can work with different input values.
            </p>

            <p>
                For example, instead of creating separate procedures for
                different Student IDs, we can create one procedure that
                accepts StudentId as an input parameter.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         BASIC SYNTAX
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Basic Syntax
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE ProcedureName
    @@ParameterName DataType
AS
BEGIN
    SELECT *
    FROM TableName
    WHERE ColumnName = @@ParameterName;
END;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Here, <strong>@@ParameterName</strong> represents the input
                parameter passed to the Stored Procedure.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SIMPLE EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example: Student ID Parameter
            </h2>

            <p>
                Suppose we have a <strong>Students</strong> table with
                columns such as StudentId, Name, City and Age.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetStudentById
    @@StudentId INT
AS
BEGIN
    SELECT *
    FROM Students
    WHERE StudentId = @@StudentId;
END;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EXECUTE PROCEDURE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Executing a Procedure with Input Parameter
            </h2>

            <p>
                After creating the Stored Procedure, we can execute it
                by passing a value to the input parameter.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>EXEC GetStudentById @@StudentId = 101;</code></pre>

            <div class="alert alert-info mt-3">
                <strong>Explanation:</strong>
                <br />
                Here, <strong>101</strong> is passed as the value of
                the <strong>StudentId</strong> parameter.
            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         PARAMETER WITH VARCHAR
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Input Parameter with VARCHAR
            </h2>

            <p>
                Input parameters can also accept text values.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetStudentsByCity
    @@City VARCHAR(50)
AS
BEGIN
    SELECT *
    FROM Students
    WHERE City = @@City;
END;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EXECUTE VARCHAR PARAMETER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Executing VARCHAR Parameter
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>EXEC GetStudentsByCity @@City = 'Pune';</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                This procedure returns students whose city is Pune.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         MULTIPLE INPUT PARAMETERS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Multiple Input Parameters
            </h2>

            <p>
                A Stored Procedure can accept more than one input parameter.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetStudentsByAgeAndCity
    @@City VARCHAR(50),
    @@Age INT
AS
BEGIN
    SELECT *
    FROM Students
    WHERE City = @@City
      AND Age = @@Age;
END;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EXECUTE MULTIPLE PARAMETERS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Executing Multiple Parameters
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>EXEC GetStudentsByAgeAndCity
    @@City = 'Pune',
    @@Age = 22;</code></pre>

            <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
                This query returns students whose City is
                <strong>Pune</strong> and Age is <strong>22</strong>.
            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         PARAMETER NAMES
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Named Parameter Execution
            </h2>

            <p>
                Named parameters make the EXEC statement easier to
                understand.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>EXEC GetStudentsByAgeAndCity
    @@Age = 22,
    @@City = 'Pune';</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The parameters can be supplied by name, so their order
                does not need to match the procedure definition.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         DEFAULT PARAMETER VALUE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Default Parameter Value
            </h2>

            <p>
                We can also provide a default value for an input parameter.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetStudentsByCity
    @@City VARCHAR(50) = 'Pune'
AS
BEGIN
    SELECT *
    FROM Students
    WHERE City = @@City;
END;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EXECUTE DEFAULT PARAMETER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Executing Default Parameter
            </h2>

            <p>
                If we do not provide a value, SQL Server uses the default
                value.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>EXEC GetStudentsByCity;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                In this example, the procedure uses
                <strong>Pune</strong> automatically.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         PRACTICAL EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Practical Example
            </h2>

            <p>
                The following Stored Procedure accepts StudentId and
                returns the details of that particular student.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetStudentDetails
    @@StudentId INT
AS
BEGIN
    SELECT
        StudentId,
        Name,
        City,
        Age
    FROM Students
    WHERE StudentId = @@StudentId;
END;</code></pre>

            <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
                Execute:
            </h5>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>EXEC GetStudentDetails @@StudentId = 101;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-warning mb-4">

        <h4 class="fw-bold">
            Important Points
        </h4>

        <ul class="mb-0">

            <li>
                Input parameters receive values from the caller.
            </li>

            <li>
                Parameters can have different data types such as
                INT, VARCHAR, DATE and DECIMAL.
            </li>

            <li>
                A Stored Procedure can have multiple input parameters.
            </li>

            <li>
                Parameters can have default values.
            </li>

            <li>
                Input parameters make Stored Procedures reusable.
            </li>

            <li>
                Use named parameters when executing procedures for
                better readability.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-primary p-4 mb-4">

        <h2 class="fw-bold">
            Summary
        </h2>

        <p class="mb-0">
            Input Parameters allow Stored Procedures to receive values
            during execution. They make procedures flexible and reusable.
            A procedure can accept one or multiple parameters, and
            parameters can also have default values. Input parameters are
            commonly used to filter data and perform operations based on
            values supplied by the user or application.
        </p>

    </div>


